# understand-anything plugin data — knowledge graph, tour results, and scratch
# under tmp/. Machine-generated, per-operator, 76 MB on this checkout, and
# nothing under it has ever been tracked. It was not ignored, so `git add -A`
# would take it: on a synced main its ua-tour-results.json carries 14 hits of
# the forbidden project identifier, which is what run_always_gates' pre-push
# scan was red on. Once development is public a commit carrying one of those
# is world-readable immediately and force-push does not recall it.
#
# Both spellings, because the plugin picks between them at runtime:
# resolveUaDirName() returns .understand-anything when that directory already
# exists and .ua otherwise, so which one appears depends on the checkout's
# history rather than on anything we control.
.ua/
.understand-anything/

# Regenerated by the ts-backend parity test suite.
ts-backend/parity-report.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Moved to the private internal repo (D#2348 phase 1).
#
# These trees now live in fulcrumaxe/fulcrumaxe-internal, seeded at commit
# 98d4ad18a4e0d39ff209dc5513603eb99bc73e0b — a full private fork of this repo's
# entire history with identical commit SHAs, plus a top commit deleting all but
# the move set. Byte-identical content, verified by blob SHA before this removal.
#
# 1551 files move here in phase 1. archive/loop-bootstrap-snapshot-2026-08-17/
# (416 files) is deliberately RETAINED: it is the audit corpus for
# open-source/'s classification gate and that gate is its only consumer, so the
# two are one unit and both move together in phase 3.
#
# `git rm --cached` keeps the files on disk only in the checkout that runs it.
# For anyone whose HEAD still tracks these paths, pulling this commit is an
# ordinary deletion and git removes all 1551 from their working tree. Copy
# anything you want to keep aside BEFORE pulling, or clone the internal repo.
#
# Ignored so a copy restored locally cannot be re-added by a `git add -A`.
# Two narrower entries were collapsed into these parents, having become
# redundant:
#   docker/gemma4-trainer/build-assets/          -> covered by /docker/
#   wiki/{Changelog,Project-Status,PR-Index}.md  -> covered by /wiki/
#
# EVERY PATTERN IS LEADING-SLASH ANCHORED, and that is load-bearing rather than
# stylistic. A bare `name/` pattern matches a directory of that name at ANY
# depth: `templates/` (unanchored) also swallows loop-bootstrap/templates/,
# which is 6 live tracked files that have nothing to do with this move. Anchored
# patterns mean "this top-level directory" and nothing else.
#
# `archive/` is deliberately NOT here. Ignoring it wholesale would break the
# Archive Protocol for every future archival: `git mv` still works (it forces
# the add), but the mandatory archive/<name>-<date>/README.md could not be
# created — `git add` exits 1 and `git add -A` skips it SILENTLY, so a retired
# file would land tracked with its README quietly missing. The pre-existing
# narrow entry for the state-db residue is restored below instead, which is
# also what keeps backend/tui_tester_helpers.py's
# archive/tui-tester-quarantine-<date> writer behaving exactly as it did before.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/wiki/
/dashboard_tui/
/scripts/training/
/scripts/serving/
/scripts/gemma-sandbox/
/verification-report/
/docker/
/systemd/
/templates/
/pr-body-p5e.txt

# Moves with dashboard_tui/ because it tests that tree EXCLUSIVELY, and left
# behind it would not fail — it rglobs dashboard_tui/screens/, and rglob over a
# missing directory yields nothing, so its single assertion passes over an empty
# set. Measured: 21 screen files scanned before, 0 after, "1 passed" both times.
# A guard would make it skip honestly, but the test has no meaning in a tree
# without the code it inspects, so it goes with it.
/tests/test_all_datatables_have_row_cursor.py

# D#1908 migration donor databases (state-db residue) — their unique rows are
# already merged into the live store. Kept on disk (findable, per Archive
# Protocol) but never committed: 3.5 MB of binaries would otherwise become a
# permanent second copy of already-recovered data in git history. Restored from
# where it sat before this PR, now anchored; archive/ itself stays tracked and
# addable.
/archive/state-db-residue-*/

